I've been to the big room several times, and it's never been much of a
problem, the bones file there notwithstanding (that was from eating the
wrong corpse). Usually the hordes are spread out enough that you can take
them in relatively bite-size chunks. It does depend on which version you
get. This one was the BIG room, a giant square with no interior walls. One
with line of sight blockage from some of the beasties would be easier.

-4 is...not fabulous, but I guess you have no protection spent. A natural
AC of 1 is more than a little helpful. When Indy IX ascended he had a -39
AC with sub-optimally enchanted gear, and no shield at all.

>One thing I haven't really been doing in any of my games is
>investigated the full Mines.  I usually head down to Minetown, then
>back up, deciding that a Luckstone probably isn't worth near-certain
>death.  Is it worth smashing through now that I can probably deal with
>it?

Absolutely. I like luckstones myself (always bless one to keep good luck
high), and there is a fairly copious quantity of loot down there, including
the guaranteed booze potions if you get the Wine Cellar (spellbooks from
the tomb are good too...I hate getting the third one). That makes them easy
to identify, and there is a fountain in the wine cellar for convenient
dilution for converting water ---> holy water, which is a huge boon.

I also like it for the spawns. It is fairly deep down, but not oppressively
so, so you get smaller quantities of elves for good chances at
pre-enchanted elven mail/cloaks, and a good supply of daggers for throwing
ammunition. Good occurence of Poly and Magic traps, which can both be put
to good use (assuming you don't find the poly trap by stepping on it
yourself...I always do 2-3 searches before moving in the Cellar, and count
the traps as they are found until they are accounted for).

>  I imagine it's considerably more worthwhile as an Arch, since all
>those gemstones are actually useful.  I might give Arch a shot,
>although I'd miss having an easy Excalibur and that handy speed.

Well, a lawful Arch can dip for Excalibur too, they just can't use it too
well unless your god gifts you with a different longsword and unrestricts
it so you can get to basic. Also, Archs start off with speed at level 1. A
not-small bonus when trying for Protection. After a thousand turns or so, I
like to let a fairly weak critter beat on me until I get down to 5 hp or
so, and then pray. In addition to being healed, because Archs start with
under 14 hp (13), your god will boost your max a bit, as high as 18 if you
are lucky. That's a decisive edge until you can hit minetown.

